body:
I am going to close this as completed, as I've addressed the issue with HashSet.

-----

Your comment regarding strings is unaddressed. I did a quick verification with two simple tests:

```csharp
[Fact]
public void Strings_1()
{
    Assert.Equal("foo", "FOO", StringComparer.InvariantCulture);
}

[Fact]
public void Strings_2()
{
    Assert.Equal("foo", "FOO", StringComparer.InvariantCultureIgnoreCase);
}
```

Strings_1 fails as expected, and Strings_2 passes as expected. Without knowing what strings you think should be passing and/or failing here, it's impossible for me to repro the problem. If you open a new issue for your string issue, please provide a full repro. Thanks!

Thanks @bradwilson, fix verified in `2.5.1-pre.9` (from https://www.myget.org/F/xunit/api/v3/index.json).

PD: You did guess the [`ToBitString()` implementation](https://github.com/eduherminio/SheepTools/blob/main/src/SheepTools/Extensions/BitArrayExtensions.cs#L23-L26) correctly, sorry for not providing it straight away:

```csharp
    public static string ToBitString(this BitArray bitArray)
    {
        return string.Join("", bitArray.Cast<bool>().Select(bit => bit ? 1 : 0));
    }
```

@bradwilson A simple test case should be:
```
Assert.Equal("ééé", "e\u0301e\u0301e\u0301");
```
This should succeed in 2.4.2 but fail in 2.5.0 (because the underlying code does `Equal(expected, actual, GetEqualityComparer<T>());`). Reading your comments I expect that it will succeed with your fix.

> That doesn't call `Assert.Equal<T>`, it calls the special string overload.

You are right, sorry. I had to re-check our code as it was not written by me. The code is rather complex (it's part of a test that parses json and compares the result to the expected result) and I over-simplified. The following should reproduce the situation:
```
string expectedValue = "e\u0301e\u0301e\u0301";
object propertyValue= "ééé";
Assert.Equal(expectedValue, propertyValue);
```
